# rpi-setup settings - one file for every task.
#
#   1. bash setup.sh --init-config     copies this file to config/rpi-setup.env
#   2. edit config/rpi-setup.env       fill in what you want, leave the rest
#   3. sudo bash setup.sh <task ...>   splits it into config/local/<task>.env
#                                      (one file per task) and runs the tasks
#
# config/rpi-setup.env is ignored by git, so your passwords stay on the Pi.
#
# Format: one NAME=value per line, as in a shell script but nothing is
# expanded ($ and backslashes are kept as written).
#   - An empty value (NAME=) means "use the default" shown in the comment.
#   - Quote a value that contains spaces followed by #, e.g. PASS='a #1'.
#     Use single quotes if the value contains a double quote, and vice versa.
#   - Lines starting with # are comments. A line can be left out entirely.
#   - yes/no settings also accept true/false, on/off and 1/0.
#   - Every name starts with its task's name; an unknown name stops the run,
#     so a typo never goes unnoticed.
#   - A variable given on the command line wins over this file:
#       sudo SAMBA_PASSWORD=secret bash setup.sh samba
#
# Passwords left empty are generated on first install, printed once and saved
# (root-only) in /var/lib/rpi-setup/secrets/<task>.env.

# --- base: OS update, firmware, SSH, fail2ban ---------------------------------
# Run "apt-get upgrade" (skipped in containers/CI). Default: yes
BASE_UPGRADE=yes
# Update the bootloader EEPROM (applies after a reboot). Default: yes
BASE_EEPROM_UPDATE=yes
# New host name, e.g. homepi (reachable as homepi.local). Empty: keep the current one.
BASE_HOSTNAME=
# Time zone, e.g. Europe/Berlin (list: timedatectl list-timezones). Empty: keep.
BASE_TIMEZONE=
# More apt packages to install, separated by spaces or commas, e.g. "rsync jq".
BASE_EXTRA_PACKAGES=
# Install security updates automatically every day (unattended-upgrades,
# Debian security archive only). Default: yes
BASE_AUTO_UPDATES=yes
# Reboot on its own at 03:30 when an automatic update needs it. Default: no
BASE_AUTO_REBOOT=no
# SSH password login. "no" allows key login only and needs your key on the Pi
# first, in ~/.ssh/authorized_keys or from the flash scripts (the task refuses otherwise). Root login over
# SSH is always switched off. Default: yes
BASE_SSH_PASSWORD_AUTH=yes
# Disk space the system journal may use, e.g. 50M or 1G; "no" keeps the
# journald default. Saves SD card space and writes. Default: 100M
BASE_JOURNAL_MAX_SIZE=100M
# Raspberry Pi 5 only (ignored on other boards; both apply after a reboot):
# PCIe Gen 3 for an NVMe HAT (twice the speed; not officially certified). Default: no
BASE_PCIE_GEN3=no
# Boot the 4K-page kernel instead of the Pi 5's 16K-page one. Only needed if a
# program or container crashes with page-size or jemalloc errors. Default: no
BASE_PI5_4K_KERNEL=no

# --- docker: Docker Engine and Compose ---------------------------------------
# Add the user who runs sudo to the docker group (docker without sudo). Default: yes
DOCKER_ADD_USER=yes

# --- network: fixed LAN address -------------------------------------------------
# Fixed IPv4 address with prefix, e.g. 192.168.1.10/24 (pick one outside your
# router's DHCP range). Empty: keep DHCP; the task prints the current address
# and how to reserve it in the router instead. Over SSH the session drops when
# the address changes: run this task alone or last, then reconnect.
NETWORK_STATIC_IP=
# Interface to configure, e.g. eth0 or wlan0. Empty: the default route's.
NETWORK_INTERFACE=
# Gateway (your router), e.g. 192.168.1.1. Empty: the current default gateway.
NETWORK_GATEWAY=
# DNS servers, comma separated. Empty: the gateway. Use 127.0.0.1 to let this
# Pi use its own Pi-hole (only once the pihole task is installed).
NETWORK_DNS=

# --- tailscale: VPN --------------------------------------------------------------
# yes: run Tailscale in a Docker container (state in /opt/tailscale) instead of
# the native package; a native tailscaled is stopped and its state copied, so no
# new login. Not recommended: if Docker breaks, remote access goes with it, and
# Tailscale SSH (TAILSCALE_SSH) cannot work from a container.
# no: native Tailscale (a container from an earlier run is stopped). Default: no
TAILSCALE_DOCKER=no
# Docker image for TAILSCALE_DOCKER=yes. Default: tailscale/tailscale:latest
TAILSCALE_IMAGE=tailscale/tailscale:latest
# Auth key from https://login.tailscale.com/admin/settings/keys for an
# unattended login. Empty: "tailscale up" prints a login URL to open.
TAILSCALE_AUTHKEY=
# Name of this Pi in your tailnet. Empty: the Pi's host name.
TAILSCALE_HOSTNAME=
# yes/no: Tailscale SSH. Empty: leave as it is.
TAILSCALE_SSH=
# yes/no: offer this Pi as an exit node (turns on IP forwarding). Empty: leave as it is.
TAILSCALE_ADVERTISE_EXIT_NODE=
# LAN subnets to reach through this Pi, comma separated, e.g. 192.168.1.0/24.
# Empty: leave as it is. (Approve routes and exit nodes in the Tailscale admin console.)
TAILSCALE_ADVERTISE_ROUTES=
# yes/no: use the tailnet's DNS settings (MagicDNS). Empty: yes, except on a Pi
# that runs Pi-hole, where it is turned off so the Pi does not resolve through itself.
TAILSCALE_ACCEPT_DNS=

# --- pihole: network-wide ad blocker --------------------------------------------
# Web admin password. Empty: generated on first install (printed and saved).
# Set it later to change the password on the next run.
PIHOLE_PASSWORD=
# Network interface Pi-hole answers on, e.g. eth0 or wlan0. Empty: the default route's.
PIHOLE_INTERFACE=
# Upstream DNS servers, comma separated. Applied at install; later change them in the web UI.
PIHOLE_DNS=1.1.1.1,1.0.0.1
# yes/no: log DNS queries. Applied at install. Default: yes
PIHOLE_QUERY_LOGGING=yes
# Port of the web admin. Empty: 80, or 8080 if another web server already has 80.
PIHOLE_WEB_PORT=
# yes: answer DNS queries from any network, e.g. your tailnet (only safe with a
# firewall in front); no: only from local subnets. Empty: leave as it is.
PIHOLE_LISTEN_ALL=
# yes: run Pi-hole in its own Docker container (data in /opt/pihole) instead of
# the installer; a native Pi-hole is stopped and its lists, settings and password
# copied over. The settings above then apply on every run (read-only in the web
# UI).
# no: native Pi-hole (a container from an earlier run is stopped). Default: no
PIHOLE_DOCKER=no
# Docker image for PIHOLE_DOCKER=yes. Default: pihole/pihole:latest
PIHOLE_IMAGE=pihole/pihole:latest

# --- samba: NAS share ---------------------------------------------------------
# Linux user who owns the share and logs in to it. Empty: the user who runs sudo.
SAMBA_USER=
# SMB password of that user. Empty: generated the first time (printed and saved);
# later runs keep the existing password unless you set one here.
SAMBA_PASSWORD=
# Share name, as in \\<pi>\nas-share. Default: nas-share
SAMBA_SHARE_NAME=nas-share
# Folder to share. Empty: <home of SAMBA_USER>/<SAMBA_SHARE_NAME>
SAMBA_SHARE_PATH=
# yes/no: read-only share. Default: no
SAMBA_READ_ONLY=no
# yes: run Samba in its own Docker container (settings in /opt/samba; the share
# folder stays where it is) instead of the apt package; a native smbd is stopped.
# Moving from a native smbd needs SAMBA_PASSWORD once (its hash cannot be copied).
# no: native Samba (a container from an earlier run is stopped). Default: no
SAMBA_DOCKER=no
# Docker image for SAMBA_DOCKER=yes. Default: crazymax/samba:latest
SAMBA_IMAGE=crazymax/samba:latest

# --- backup: nightly archive of rpi-setup's data and settings -----------------
# Folder for the archives (they hold passwords, so they are root-only). Use a USB
# disk, e.g. /mnt/usb/rpi-setup: a copy on the SD card dies with the SD card.
# Default: /var/backups/rpi-setup
BACKUP_DEST=/var/backups/rpi-setup
# Number of archives to keep; older ones are deleted. Default: 7
BACKUP_KEEP=7
# When to run, as a systemd OnCalendar time, e.g. 03:00 or 'Sun 04:30'.
# A run missed while the Pi was off happens at the next boot. Default: 03:00
BACKUP_TIME=03:00
# yes/no: also back up the Samba share's files (can be big). Default: no
BACKUP_INCLUDE_SHARE=no

# --- runner: manual Deploy workflow from your private repository ------------------
# A GitHub Actions runner on the Pi, registered to a PRIVATE repository of
# yours that holds your settings and a "Deploy to Pi" workflow
# (template: templates/private-repo/). Never use a public repository here.
# Your private repository as owner/name, e.g. Chriszly/rpi-private. Required.
RUNNER_REPO=
# One-time registration token: in the private repository open Settings >
# Actions > Runners > New self-hosted runner and copy the value after
# --token. Valid for one hour; not needed once the runner is registered.
RUNNER_TOKEN=
# Runner name shown at GitHub. Empty: the Pi's hostname.
RUNNER_NAME=
# Comma separated labels the workflow picks the Pi by. Empty: RUNNER_NAME.
RUNNER_LABELS=
# Branches of this repository a deploy may switch to, space or comma
# separated. Default: main
RUNNER_BRANCHES=main

# --- web: nginx ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Port nginx listens on. Empty: 80, or 8080 if Pi-hole already has 80.
WEB_PORT=
# Title of the start page (/var/www/html/index.html; a page of your own is never overwritten).
WEB_TITLE=Raspberry Pi
# yes: run nginx in its own Docker container (files in /opt/web/html) instead of
# the apt package; a native nginx is stopped and its /var/www/html copied over.
# no: native nginx (a container from an earlier run is stopped). Default: no
WEB_DOCKER=no
# Docker image for WEB_DOCKER=yes. Default: nginx:stable-alpine
WEB_IMAGE=nginx:stable-alpine

# --- monitoring: Netdata dashboard ------------------------------------------------
# Dashboard port. Default: 19999
MONITORING_PORT=19999
# Address to listen on: 0.0.0.0 = whole LAN, 127.0.0.1 = this Pi only. Default: 0.0.0.0
MONITORING_BIND=0.0.0.0
# yes/no: send Netdata's anonymous usage statistics. Default: no
MONITORING_TELEMETRY=no
# yes: run Netdata in its own Docker container (data in /opt/monitoring) instead
# of the apt package; a native netdata is stopped (its metric history stays behind).
# no: native netdata (a container from an earlier run is stopped). Default: no
MONITORING_DOCKER=no
# Docker image for MONITORING_DOCKER=yes. Default: netdata/netdata:stable
MONITORING_IMAGE=netdata/netdata:stable

# --- netalertx: LAN device tracker (needs docker) ------------------------------
# Web UI port. Default: 20211
NETALERTX_PORT=20211
# Subnets to scan, e.g. '192.168.1.0/24 --interface=eth0'; several separated by ";".
# Empty: detected from the default route.
NETALERTX_SCAN_SUBNETS=
# yes/no: ask for a password before the web UI opens. no = anyone on the LAN can open it. Default: yes
NETALERTX_LOGIN=yes
# Web UI password. Empty: generated on the first install, printed once and saved in
# /var/lib/rpi-setup/secrets/netalertx.env (re-runs keep using it).
NETALERTX_PASSWORD=
# Docker image. Default: ghcr.io/netalertx/netalertx:latest
NETALERTX_IMAGE=ghcr.io/netalertx/netalertx:latest

# --- teamspeak: TeamSpeak 6 server (needs docker, 64-bit OS) --------------------
# yes: you accept the TeamSpeak server license; the server does not start without it.
TEAMSPEAK_ACCEPT_LICENSE=yes
# Voice port (UDP) on the Pi; can be changed later (the server keeps its own port inside). Default: 9987
TEAMSPEAK_VOICE_PORT=9987
# File transfer port (TCP). Default: 30033
TEAMSPEAK_FILE_PORT=30033
# yes/no: web query API, and its port (TCP). Default: yes, 10080
TEAMSPEAK_QUERY_HTTP=yes
TEAMSPEAK_QUERY_PORT=10080
# Password of the serveradmin query account. Empty: TeamSpeak's own (none set).
TEAMSPEAK_QUERY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# Docker image. Default: teamspeaksystems/teamspeak6-server:latest
TEAMSPEAK_IMAGE=teamspeaksystems/teamspeak6-server:latest

# --- firewall: nftables, only what the services need (run it last) --------------
# Own table "inet rpi_setup", loaded at boot by rpi-setup-firewall.service; Docker's
# and Tailscale's rules are left alone. Always allowed: SSH, ping, loopback,
# Tailscale (tailscale0), Docker networks, mDNS (<host>.local) and replies.
# Re-run "setup.sh firewall" after installing another task to open its ports.
# Turn it off with: sudo systemctl disable --now rpi-setup-firewall
# Note: ports published by Docker containers (e.g. TeamSpeak's) are forwarded
# by Docker itself and are not limited by this firewall.
# SSH port, always allowed (ports sshd really listens on are allowed too). Default: 22
FIREWALL_SSH_PORT=22
# yes: open the ports of the rpi-setup services installed on this Pi (Pi-hole,
# nginx, Netdata, NetAlertX, TeamSpeak, Samba, Tailscale). no: only SSH and
# FIREWALL_EXTRA_PORTS. Default: yes
FIREWALL_AUTO_PORTS=yes
# More ports to open to anyone, port/proto or from-to/proto, e.g. "8123/tcp 1900/udp".
FIREWALL_EXTRA_PORTS=
# Subnets allowed to reach the web UIs (Pi-hole admin, nginx, Netdata, NetAlertX),
# e.g. 192.168.1.0/24; several separated by spaces. Tailscale is always allowed.
# Empty: anyone.
FIREWALL_ALLOW_FROM=

# --- flash: SD card first-boot settings (host/flash.sh, host/flash.ps1) ---------
# Read on the PC that writes the card, not by setup.sh on the Pi. Remove the "#"
# in front of the lines you use. Flags and environment variables win over these.
# With none of them set the card is written exactly as before (user and SSH only).
# Host name, e.g. homepi (reachable as homepi.local). Empty: raspberrypi.
#FLASH_HOSTNAME=
# Wi-Fi network to join on first boot. Empty: no Wi-Fi (use Ethernet).
#FLASH_WIFI_SSID=
# Its password (8-63 characters). Empty: asked for while flashing.
#FLASH_WIFI_PASSWORD=
# Wi-Fi country code (regulatory domain). Default: DE
#FLASH_WIFI_COUNTRY=DE
# SSH public key to authorize for the login user, e.g. /home/you/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
# (Windows: C:\Users\you\.ssh\id_ed25519.pub). Empty: password login only.
#FLASH_SSH_PUBKEY_FILE=
# yes: create a new SSH key for the Pi to use with GitHub; its public key is
# printed at the end to add on GitHub. Empty: yes when run at a terminal.
#FLASH_GITHUB_KEY=
